WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Three dials and a budget.

2
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:09.283
When a session is too slow, too expensive, or not good enough, there are three things you can change, and they are easy to conflate.

3
00:00:09.563 --> 00:00:17.797
The tool is the harness: Claude Code, Codex, Copilot, Cursor, Gemini CLI or its successor Antigravity CLI.

4
00:00:18.077 --> 00:00:27.123
It decides what tools the model has, how context is managed, what is enforced, and where the work runs (your machine, a cloud sandbox, a CI runner).

5
00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:30.411
Switching tools changes the loop, not just the model.

6
00:00:30.691 --> 00:00:32.248
The model is the reasoner.

7
00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:41.595
Each vendor offers tiers: a most-capable model, a cheaper near-peer for everyday work, and small fast models for simple, high-volume jobs.

8
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:45.800
The effort level controls how much the model deliberates before acting.

9
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.861
In Claude Code it runs from low to max, set with /effort or per skill or subagent.

10
00:00:52.141 --> 00:00:55.491
Then there’s the budget: money, your time, and your attention.

11
00:00:55.771 --> 00:00:59.056
A cheaper setup that needs twice the review is not cheaper.

12
00:00:59.336 --> 00:01:09.853
This lesson gives you starting points for each dial, honest about how thin the public evidence is, and then the method that actually settles it: a small comparison on your own work.
